A) IInnttrroodduuccttiioonn
The Census is the official count of population, dwellings and households in Vanuatu and it provides a ‘snapshot’
of the country at one specific point in time: midnight 16th November 2020. The Census provides a unique source
of detailed demographic, social and economic information about the whole population of Vanuatu. This
information is used for policy making and planning, monitoring and evaluation, research and other decision
making. There are many users of statistics from the Census including government agencies like the Ministry of
Finance and Economic Management for resource allocation, the Ministry of Education and Youth Development
and Training for the population in each of the school catchment areas as well as population projections for the
school population of the future, the Ministry of Health uses the population, fertility and household amenity
information for a number of purposes in its planning and decision making as well as monitoring and evaluation.
Students are avid users of Census data for the many research projects they undertake. The Census provides
information for local government (Province, Area Council) in its activities as well as numerous private
researchers and their subjects of interest. The Census data is also available in Geographic Information System
output format which is used for agricultural land use planning when used in conjunction with other map layers
such as type of topsoil, and patterns of population distribution used for transport and other infrastructure
planning.
B) AA  bbrriieeff  hhiissttoorryy  ooff  CCeennssuuss  ttaakkiinngg  iinn  VVaannuuaattuu
A full census has taken place in Vanuatu every 10 years since 1979. Unlike many other Pacific countries,
Vanuatu does not have a long history of census taking. The first National Population Census took place in 1967,
followed by another in 1979. The first official population Census in 1967 enumerated over 70,000 New
Hebrideans (today referred to as Ni‐Vanuatu) and 5,745 other people of different ethnic groups.
The second national population census was in 1979, a year before the condominium’s
independence. Three other full national censuses have been conducted since the country’s independence in
1980. The first census after independence took place on the 16th of May 1989 with the theme “The First National
Population Census of Vanuatu conducted by Ni‐Vanuatu”. 

