Census information technology
For the 2020 Census the VNSO took part in two regional initiatives: one involving the use of GPS (Global
Positioning System) technology to map individual households and the second to improve the efficiency of data
collection by using android tablets programmed with a data collection software. The use of tablets has been first
used as trial in the 2016 mini census and proved effective. The VNSO has taken up this initiative to continue with
the use of tablets in all its surveys and censuses. Regardless of this sudden shift in data collection method-from
paper form to the use of tablets- there are pros and cons in both methods that the census team has to ensure is
managed properly to safeguard the data that is collected in the field.
Questionnaire Development
The 2020 Census questionnaire was designed to ensure consistency with the 2009 Census statistical
information while meeting regional standards for ‘model’ questionnaire and meeting international statistical
requirements. The Census Pre‐test survey was conducted in 3 provinces during June 2020, precisely the same
year of the census actual collection. For the urban areas, three areas in Port Vila and 3 areas in the Peri-urban
on the outskirt of Port Vila and five areas in the rural of Efate Island: The area known as Blacksands on the
outskirts of Port Vila and five areas around Luganville town and others were selected as the developing areas
representing peri‐urban areas, and for the rural areas, Tanna island in Tafea province was selected for inclusion
in the Pre‐test. The Pre‐test provided the opportunity to test both the questionnaires in terms of question content
and order of questions and the Census data collection using the tablets to synchronize the data back to Port Vila
using the Survey Solution software.
The Pre‐test included a number of ‘new’ questions compared to that used in 2009. Based onsubmissions by
users and follow‐up discussions about their information needs, questions ondisability, mortality, literacy, the main
source of household income, if a person had worked overseas in the last 12 months, the use of mobile phones,
and small businesses operated by households were tested and refined.
Based on lessons learnt from the Pre‐test, other household surveys and the 2009 Census forms, all questions
were programmed into the tablets includin

