completed in most areas of the country within two weeks. In areas that were particularly difficult to reach, or
where transport was difficult to arrange and, in some cases, not available at all, the enumeration took longer.
A Post Enumeration Survey (PES) planned for two weeks after the completion of enumeration did not eventuate
as some areas were still conducting interviews and the timing was inconvenient because of the festive season
(Christmas and New Year).  Furthermore, there was a lot people moving between communities at that time so
the objective of the PES to re‐count household members to verify Census counts would have been difficult to
achieve. Vanuatu has never conducted a PES and one of the contributing factors is the capacity as requirements
of PES is to have independent bodies conducting such activity, other than VNSO itself
Census data processing
The use of the tablets in the data collection during the 2020 census did apply same steps of data checks. This
time, the interviewer was provided the opportunity to check his or her own work by going through what s/he has
entered into the tablets to see if all questions have been answered correctly. The checked questions are then
sent to the specific team Supervisors tablets using the Digicel data network. The supervisor then checked the
incoming questionnaire for any mistakes. If a supervisor identified a mistake, he or she would make a comment
on the specific question and send the questionnaire back to the respective enumerator to amend the error.
If the supervisor checks the questions and no error is identified, the supervisor sends the checked question to his
or her respective HQ supervisor to go through the questionnaire again for any errors the supervisors might have
overlooked. If no error is identified by the HQ supervisor, this question is being approved the HQ supervisor and
stored away in the data awaiting to be worked on after the census enumeration.
In order to obtain accurate statistics from household-based surveys such as the Census, the data must be as
free as possible from errors and inconsistencies. Statistics derived from 'dirty data' (that is, data which contains
errors) may produce an inaccurate profile of the country or geographic area.
